# FastConformer CTC: NeMo vs. TensorRT Benchmark

This guide prepares LibriSpeech `test-clean`, benchmarks the original NeMo
FastConformer CTC Large model, exports it to ONNX/TensorRT, and compares the
two runtimes using both random-order and duration-sorted manifests.

## Installation

```bash
docker run -it --gpus '"device=0"' --ipc=host -v $PWD:/home nvcr.io/nvidia/nemo:25.11

pip install pycuda
```

## Prerequisites

Run every command from this directory. The following files are expected to be
available here:

- `convert_librispeech_to_nemo.py`
- `filter_manifest.py`
- `sort_manifest.py`
- `transcribe_speech_file.py`
- `export_fastconformer_onnx.py`
- `build_trt_engine.sh`
- `benchmark_fastconformer_encoder_trt_file.py`

The NeMo checkpoint is referenced as:

```text
./stt_en_fastconformer_ctc_large.nemo
```

## 1. Prepare LibriSpeech test-clean

Download and extract the dataset:

```bash
wget https://openslr.trmal.net/resources/12/test-clean.tar.gz
tar -xzf test-clean.tar.gz
```

Convert the LibriSpeech metadata into a NeMo manifest:

```bash
python convert_librispeech_to_nemo.py \
  --librispeech_dir /home/fastconformer/trt_fastconformer/LibriSpeech \
  --output_manifest test_clean_manifest.json \
  --subset test-clean
```

Remove samples longer than 20 seconds:

```bash
python filter_manifest.py \
  --input_manifest test_clean_manifest.json \
  --output_manifest test_clean_manifest_filtered.json \
  --max_duration 20.0
```

Create a duration-ascending version of the filtered manifest. This is the
sorted workload used for more efficient batching:

```bash
python sort_manifest.py \
  --input_manifest test_clean_manifest_filtered.json \
  --output_manifest test_clean_manifest_sorted_filtered.json
```

The two benchmark inputs are:

| Workload | Manifest |
| --- | --- |
| Random | `test_clean_manifest_filtered.json` |
| Sorted | `test_clean_manifest_sorted_filtered.json` |

## 2. Benchmark the NeMo model

Download the nemo model:

```bash
wget https://huggingface.co/nvidia/stt_en_fastconformer_ctc_large/resolve/main/stt_en_fastconformer_ctc_large.nemo
```

Run the baseline on the duration-sorted manifest (check with different batch sizes):

```bash
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python transcribe_speech_file.py \
  --model ./stt_en_fastconformer_ctc_large.nemo \
  --manifest test_clean_manifest_sorted_filtered.json \
  --batch-size 64 \
  --warmup-steps 5 \
  --run-steps 10 \
  --device cuda
```

Repeat the same command for the random manifest by changing `--manifest` to
`test_clean_manifest_filtered.json`. To reproduce the comparison below, run
both manifests with batch sizes `32`, `64`, and `128`.

## 3. Export NeMo to ONNX and build TensorRT

Export the FastConformer encoder to ONNX:

```bash
python3 export_fastconformer_onnx.py \
  --model ./stt_en_fastconformer_ctc_large.nemo \
  --output fastconformer_ctc_large.onnx
```

Build the FP16 TensorRT engine:

```bash
bash build_trt_engine.sh
```

This produces `fastconformer_encoder_fp16.plan`.

## 4. Benchmark the TensorRT engine

Example TensorRT run for the sorted workload at batch size 128:

```bash
python3 benchmark_fastconformer_encoder_trt_file.py \
  --engine fastconformer_encoder_fp16.plan \
  --nemo ./stt_en_fastconformer_ctc_large.nemo \
  --manifest test_clean_manifest_sorted_filtered.json \
  --batch-sizes 1 8 32 64 128 \
  --warmup 5 \
  --runs 10 \
  --csv results.csv
```

Repeat with `--manifest test_clean_manifest_filtered.json` for the random
workload. Benchmark batch sizes `32`, `64`, and `128` (the engine supports
batch sizes up to 128).

## Results

All results use five warm-up iterations and ten measured iterations. RTFX is
real-time factor: larger is better.

GPU: 1xA100

| Batch size | Mean batch latency (NeMo random / TRT random) | Mean batch latency (NeMo sorted / TRT sorted) | Random RTFX (NeMo / TRT) | Sorted RTFX (NeMo / TRT) | RTFX speedup (random / sorted) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 32 | 251.56 ms / 18.42 ms | 114.77 ms / 20.31 ms | 868.14x / 4,124.90x | 1,902.77x / 3,742.10x | 4.75x / 1.97x |
| 64 | 472.62 ms / 23.02 ms | 192.82 ms / 26.17 ms | 924.16x / 7,768.23x | 2,265.26x / 6,832.99x | 8.41x / 3.02x |
| 128 | 1,026.64 ms / 44.56 ms | 374.88 ms / 45.00 ms | 850.89x / 10,630.59x | 2,330.26x / 10,525.53x | 12.49x / 4.52x |

TensorRT delivers the largest random-workload gain at batch size 128: **12.49x**
RTFX over NeMo. Sorted input materially improves the NeMo baseline, while the
TensorRT engine remains consistently high-throughput.
